SM(603,{"n":"Hebrews 11.1-16","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Hebrews 11.1-16.mp3","d":3139.2,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.56,"Okay, we'll go ahead and get started."],[22.72,"And when we get started in just a moment, we'll be picking up in uh St."],[26.32,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, uh chapter 11."],[30.64,"Uh we left off last time at the end of uh chapter 10."],[36.88,"Uh we've talked about how St."],[38.4,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews is a little different than his other epistles, in that, first of all, it's not really an epistle in the sense that it's not really a letter, but that it's more of like a homily or a sermon uh that has been sent somewhere."],[53.54,"And so we're gonna see eventually when we get to the end in chapter 13 that there's sort of the stuff you'd expect at the end of a letter, but we saw back at the beginning of chapter one, there wasn't any of the stuff you'd expect at the beginning of a letter."],[67.04,"Because it's a text that's being sent somewhere."],[70.0,"Um we're gonna see tonight again some more of that evidence of this being something that was delivered orally, to people."],[78.97,"We've already seen some of that uh as we've gone along."],[83.37,"And so uh one of the other recurring themes, and this is part of where we ended up last time, has been a series of warnings against apostasy, warnings against leaving the Christian faith."],[96.43,"Turning away from Christ, uh, either under persecution and difficulty, or just uh because. of the things of the world, or for whatever other reason"],[107.75,"And so this contrast has been made, and we talked about how it's usually in English translations like the New King James Version that we're reading from in the Orthodox Study Bible, um, that it's usually translated with the word faith, which is of course a vague word, and especially if you're one of our Protestant friends, allows you to read a lot of theology uh into various sentences, that as we've seen isn't necessarily there."],[136.95,"Um, but um this uh idea of faithfulness, as we've argued, it would be better translated, has been what is contrasted with apostasy and turning away."],[151.12,"And that makes a lot more sense than trying to contrast, on one hand, believing that something is true, and on the other hand, falling into sin and turning away from Christ."],[162.9,"One is sort of if you make one sort of an intellectual thing, and then the other one is a more holistic thing that doesn't make sense."],[169.56,"It makes sense to contrast a holistic thing with right, faithfulness and unfaithfulness."],[176.05,"Faithfulness and betrayal."],[177.67,"These are things that are natural opposites."],[180.41,"And so last the last chapter, chapter 10, was another one of these, it's sort of a very serious and dire warning against turning away from Christ."],[191.33,"And then that was contrasted at the end with faithfulness and in the context of remaining faithful until the end."],[200.95,"Of endurance, of patience, enduring sufferings and difficulties."],[205.53,"This is another thing that brings out that idea of faithfulness."],[209.78,"Over against just believing something is true all the way up until you die."],[216.5,"That this is about faithfulness in the face of those things."],[220.26,"And so now, um, as we get into chapter 11, chapter 11 is where St."],[228.18,"Paul is going to now expound on what faithfulness is."],[233.32,"What faithfulness looks like."],[234.2,"Because it's one thing to talk about that in the abstract"],[240.44,"And we talked about how one of the ways that idea is brought out in the Old Testament is that uh the relationship between God and his people, Israel or Judah or whomever at the time, is talked about, is often talked about in terms of like a marriage."],[259.66,"And so the faithfulness of the two parties, the faithfulness of God to his people, and the faithfulness or lack thereof of his people to God is put into these sort of marriage like terms of being faithful or unfaithful."],[274.43,"But even that could be an abstract thing."],[277.29,"Because what does it mean for me to be faithful or loyal to God when I'm driving my car right down the street, or I'm working at a job, or I'm sleeping, or I'm watching Netflix."],[292.35,"Like what is what does that concretely mean?"],[296.99,"And so um St."],[300.75,"Paul is now going to be sort of the reason he's now going to kind of define that is he wants to explain here's what that means."],[308.67,"And as we're gonna see in chapter 11, the primary way he's going to do that is by using examples from the Old Testament."],[316.29,"He's gonna go back to the story of these Old Testament figures, uh, particularly in the book of Genesis, but then also he's going to get into Exodus and brush up against."],[330.57,"He's going to get Joshua then sort of brush up against Judges and not go much further than that."],[337.05,"But he's going to focus on these stories and show these figures who showed faithfulness."],[344.51,"It in some circumstance or in their lives as a whole, to serve as an example of uh to us."],[353.23,"And it's important that we realize what he's doing."],[357.83,"Because this is one of the places where what we believe is Orthodox Christians sometimes rubs up against our a.forementioned Protestant friends"],[367.07,"Because one of the things they tend to have a problem with in our beliefs is the whole idea of saints."],[374.97,"Of us venerating saints, talking about saints, having icons of saints, singing a hymn about a saint."],[381.95,"And the way that's often expressed is well, why don't you just pray directly to Christ?"],[389.39,"Why don't why would you have icons of saints and not just Christ?"],[392.61,"Why the Theotokos and not just Christ?"],[395.35,"Why not just him?"],[397.67,"And uh while there's a certain logic there, of like, well, if you could talk to God directly, why wouldn't you just do that?"],[408.03,"There is a certain logic there."],[409.55,"Now that's a logic that often those same people aren't consistent with because they'll ask their pastor to pray for them, they'll ask their friends to pray for them, and you could equally ask them, well, why are you asking them to pray for you?"],[420.83,"Why don't you just pray to God yourself?"],[422.59,"You could go directly to God."],[424.56,"But um that inconsistency, set aside, that isn't the example that we see in the scriptures."],[434.19,"So we have the Old Testament as part of our scriptures."],[438.25,"Now I think the Old Testament is speaking to us about Christ."],[443.66,"But most of the Old Testament, Christ is not in the narrative the main character."],[447.98,"the main In the Gospels, obviously Christ is in the narrative character."],[455.06,"He is the main person."],[458.5,"But when you read Genesis, you read stories about Adam and Cain and Abel."],[465.33,"And Christ is very much there."],[467.67,"And he's part of that story."],[469.91,"He's to be found in that story, but it's following these other people."],[476.31,"So the question, well, why don't you just have Christ?"],[479.35,"Why do you have these other people?"],[480.63,"One answer they gotta be, well, why don't we just have the Gospels?"],[484.79,"Why do we have the rest of the scriptures?"],[489.57,"Why do we have the book of Acts?"],[491.21,"Why do we need to read about St."],[492.49,"Peter and St."],[493.45,"Paul and St."],[495.05,"Philip and these other people?"],[497.47,"Why not just have the gospels?"],[498.91,"Why do we need to read about all these people of the Old Testament?"],[502.67,"All this crazy stuff, violent stuff they did."],[505.52,"Why don't we just read the Gospels and just read by the life of Jesus?"],[509.89,"And even more so."],[513.15,"We're gonna get to a text at the end of chapter 11 that always comes up when people talk about saints."],[520.75,"But the reason I'm talking about this now is it's not just that one verse right at the end of the chapter."],[527.55,"What St. Paul is doing in this whole chapter, is he's pointing to the saints of the old covenant and showing how they should serve as models for how we live the Christian life."],[546.37,"So he's doing with the Old Testament saints, with the saints who were before him, exactly the same thing that the church today does with the saints who have come before us."],[560.26,"When we're encouraged to read the lives of the saints."],[564.51,"And see how they lived the Christian life."],[567.01,"To encourage us and to show us, as an example, how we should seek to live our Christian life."],[576.85,"So the church, when talking about the saints and their lives and venerating the saints and commemorating them on particular days, where we hear about their lives and what they did, is following the example of what St."],[589.11,"Paul is doing right here."],[592.47,"And it's been an example that the church has followed all the way along since the beginning."],[609.27,"A previous departed saint, not putting them alongside Christ at the same level."],[616.17,"Not saying that person's going to save you, but talking about their life and how they lived the life in Christ. Christ as a way of helping us come to know more deeply"],[628.03,"That example gets followed."],[629.88,"And of course, even before that, every time a Christian preacher preached about St."],[636.84,"Peter or Saint Paul from the book of Acts, every time they were talking about preceding saints, not apart from Christ, but as continuing to lead us to Christ just as they did when they were with us alive in the material world."],[654.76,"So all that said, and introduced and set up."],[658.26,"Unless anybody has any leftover questions com,ments, stock prices."],[663.7,"We'll go ahead and pick up in St"],[670.3,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, chapter 11, verse 1."],[677.82,"Now, faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen, for by it the elders obtained a good testimony."],[690.38,"So the English translation here has done the opposite of make this make any sense."],[698.19,"So um a couple things."],[700.35,"Now we've already said, obviously, faith is better translated faithfulness."],[704.95,"And the word that's translated. substance there is the word uh hypostasis"],[710.67,"That's the word that's used for person when we talk about the doctrine of the Trinity."],[715.07,"We say that God is one in essence, one in nature, three persons."],[721.71,"We're saying three hypostases, the same word."],[724.77,"That's here translated as substance."],[727.49,"And what that word means in Greek is a a particular being."],[737.22,"Being the way we talk about entity."],[740.08,"Entity being uh sort of a particular localized right thing."],[746.48,"So, what is this saying? about hope"],[752.06,"So, what have we read in Hebrews so far about hope?"],[757.82,"Hope has surrounded, even at the end of the last chapter."],[761.58,"Christ's return. Our glorification, eternal life."],[765.85,"That's the hope."],[766.73,"That's the thing we're hoping for at the end of our lives."],[770.19,"And so, St."],[771.47,"Paul is here saying that faithfulness is the substance of that, the reality of that."],[779.95,"So, what does that mean?"],[782.09,"Obviously, this doesn't work real well for thinking something is true."],[786.19,"Believing something is true is kind of what hope is."],[790.87,"Hope.e means we believe that at the end of our lives it's tru"],[795.79,"So, how could believing something intellectually give more substance to hope?"],[803.33,"It's like the same thing as hope."],[806.85,"I hope this is true, I believe this is true."],[810.49,"So, but faithfulness, actually being faithful, and the way that we live our lives gives substance, gives reality."],[820.15,"Makes real the hope that we have."],[825.63,"And this is the exact opposite of the way we as modern Christians often think about it."],[834.56,"So we have trouble intellectually believing."],[839.78,"Or we have trouble hoping."],[842.0,"That intellectual level, believing that that hope is true."],[845.12,"And so we then want evidence from God."],[848.9,"We want something we can point to."],[850.74,"We want proof, frankly."],[852.75,"Prove to me that this is true, that there's eternal life after we die, that there's life after death at all."],[857.81,"Prove to me that it's true that Jesus is God."],[860.87,"Prove to me that it's true."],[862.07,"And then we would then we say, okay, then I will intellectually believe that it's true."],[868.09,"And put my hope in this."],[870.83,"And then at that point, then maybe I'll live a life that is faithful."],[874.67,"Paul is saying the exact opposite is the case"],[876.16,"Down the road after that."],[877.36,"And St. The way that our hope becomes certain."],[884.46,"The way we have certainty in it is by living a life that is faithful."],[890.72,"That's how we come to know Christ."],[893.76,"That's how we come to see Christ."],[896.53,"So when we're living a life following Christ, and we see living a life of repentance, as Hebrews has talked about, we're living a life where we're doing good for others, we're loving one another."],[909.88,"We'll see ourselves changing and being transformed by it."],[913.4,"We'll see the lives of other people around us being transformed by it."],[917.58,"And then that will give our hope substance."],[921.52,"Because we see it in action."],[924.58,"We see it in action."],[925.94,"That's what he's getting at here."],[929.38,"And that's then the second part of that, of verse one, is just repeating that in another way."],[935.38,"The evidence of things not seen."],[937.4,"That's the evidence."],[940.37,"That's the evidence."],[942.45,"This is sort of like what Christ says when, remember, there's that episode where St."],[947.17,"John the Forerunner is in prison awaiting his death."],[951.89,"And he sends a couple of his disciples to come see Jesus and say, Are you the one?"],[956.87,"Like, are you the Messiah?"],[958.07,"Are you the one who has promised?"],[960.03,"Or is there someone else coming after you?"],[965.28,"And Christ's response is, well, look, what do you see?"],[970.34,"The lame walk, the blind see."],[973.76,"People are having their sins forgiven."],[975.84,"All of these things, that should tell you who Christ is and what's happening."],[982.94,"And so it's the same way."],[985.36,"It's when we're faithful, and then because we're faithful, we're seeing ourselves transformed and our world transformed."],[993.28,"We're seeing God work."],[995.12,"That lets us know that it's for real."],[998.64,"We don't sit back and say, uh, I don't know I need a I need a sign right that's who those are the people who Christ is always condemning right the people who come to him and want a sign oh yeah if you're the Messiah do a magic trick right if you're Messiah do so you know do something you know and you can point to all those other things he did and say yeah yeah that's old hat what have you done for what have you done for me lately right like that's right we want to see something then we'll believe then we'll believe you're the Messiah then we'll become your followers once you prove it to us right so it's we've got it exactly in reverse."],[1037.78,"Of how we of how we become certain."],[1041.86,"So notice verse 2 also for by it the elders."],[1045.32,"Those who came before, the people he's about to talk about in detail."],[1049.01,"The people of that old covenant he's been talking about."],[1051.33,"That's how they obtained a good testimony."],[1055.09,"Remember, he's used this image repeatedly already in Hebrews of when a person dies, them coming into the presence of God, with the angels and the holy ones and the saints and Christ sort of introducing them."],[1072.33,"This sort of image of, and so that testimony, that report, is who you are, who you've become."],[1079.81,"And so he's saying it's by faithfulness that they achieved that."],[1085.26,"The people who have come before us."],[1087.22,"And now he's going to break that down in more detail."],[1090.52,"So the hope that we have now is the hope that those the saints who came before us had when they were in our place."],[1100.22,"When they were alive and working out their salvation."],[1103.36,"And so they achieved it."],[1107.38,"They achieved it by faithfulness."],[1109.3,"And so by looking at them, we can see now the model of how we can achieve that hope."],[1119.06,"So he starts now in verse 3, all the way back, the beginning of Genesis."],[1126.1,"By faith we understand that the worlds were framed by the Word of God."],[1129.94,"So that the things which are seen were not made of things which are visible"],[1138.7,"So I'm not gonna I don't want I'll try not to belabor this too much."],[1144.23,"But again, does the word faith work here?"],[1147.99,"By believing it's true, we understand that God created everything."],[1156.36,"That doesn't bring understanding."],[1157.82,"But by faithfulness, we come to understand that God is the creator of everything."],[1164.94,"That's how we come to understand it."],[1172.38,"Verse 4. Notice Adam and Eve get skipped"],[1177.46,"Why do Adam and Eve get skipped?"],[1179.62,"They're not a good example of faithfulness."],[1186.26,"Verse 4, by faith, Abel offered to God a more excellent sacrifice than Cain, through which he obtained witness that he was righteous."],[1197.14,"God testifying of his gifts, and through it, he being dead still speaks."],[1204.98,"So Abel here is sort of our first human saint."],[1210.82,"He's also the first human to die"],[1214.8,"So Abel, who's in Genesis is the first human to die."],[1220.06,"He offers a more excellent sacrifice than Cain, through which he obtained witness, that witness idea again."],[1227.42,"This testimony that he was righteous."],[1231.1,"Because it was God himself testifying of his gifts, that being his sacrifice."],[1237.62,"Because God, it says in Genesis that God found his sacrifice acceptable."],[1243.86,"He found his sacrifice acceptable."],[1249.06,"Now notice, notice."],[1253.62,"Again, I'll tringy not to labor it, but does faith work here?"],[1259.16,"Now, and again, I don't want to do this in a way that's mean or off putting, but our Protestant friends want to say, as part of their theology, that the way in which Abel became a saint."],[1272.5,"The way in which he was, the reason he was, it was testified that he was righteous, is that he had faith."],[1280.87,"What exactly that means in this context, I don't know."],[1283.51,"He believed God existed, they'd seen him."],[1286.15,"So I don't know what the faith means there."],[1288.85,"But it was just by faith."],[1290.21,"But is that what this says?"],[1293.73,"God testified about what?"],[1295.33,"His gift, the sacrifice."],[1297.89,"He did something."],[1299.49,"That's what the text just says."],[1301.01,"The text just says that the reason Abel was accounted righteous is that he did something."],[1303.97,"a He offered worthy sacrifice."],[1309.29,"And he did that faithfully."],[1313.05,"That's why he offered a worthy sacrifice is because he was faithful to God."],[1320.01,"So you can't separate in this verse, in this example that St."],[1325.69,"Paul is giving, faith, like some kind of belief."],[1329.37,"And again, it's unclear exactly what Abel would have believed to be true from the action, the work of offering a correct sacrifice that was pleasing to God."],[1344.89,"And that the action he took, the work he did of sacrifice was. pleasing to God, according to Hebrews here"],[1359.25,"Uh it's uh pistes in in in uh Greek, and different forms of that word, the noun form, the verb form, get translated as faith, faithful, faithfulness, belief, believe, believing in English."],[1379.74,"And in the New King James, even though they're all the same word."],[1383.56,"So there's some places where you just can't get around the fact that it's faithfulness."],[1387.4,"We saw one of those last time."],[1389.5,"But there are a lot of other places where, for theological reasons, translators want to use faith."],[1392.54,"Or belief Which is even further removing."],[1399.42,"Because like faith at least can carry that meaning of faithfulness."],[1404.23,"Like he kept faith with his wife."],[1406.87,"He kept faith with his duties."],[1410.73,"You can get that idea of faithfulness in there."],[1413.21,"But belief you really can't."],[1415.63,"Belief is not even related."],[1418.35,"At least in modern English."],[1420.51,"Maybe somewhere in the distant past of etymology, it was connected, but um wasn't."],[1430.27,"And then notice that last piece, again, not to pile on, but through it, he being dead still speaks so um for some of our friends who want to say the saints can't pray for people or do anything because they're dead."],[1449.45,"Here's the first one, and he still speaks."],[1457.66,"Then verse five By faith Enoch was taken away so that he did not see death and was not found because God had taken him."],[1469.02,"For before he was taken, he had this testimony that he pleased God."],[1475.74,"Does faith make sense there?"],[1477.82,"He believed something."],[1479.38,"Again, we don't know what."],[1484.9,"So by faithfulness he was taken away, so he did not see death."],[1489.62,"For because what does it mean that by faithfulness that happened?"],[1494.26,"Because before he was taken away, he had this testimony that he pleased God."],[1499.3,"He was faithful to God, he lived a life that pleased God, God took him without him dying."],[1505.46,"Very clear, that's what it says."],[1510.18,"Verse 6. But without faith it is impossible to please him."],[1516.02,"For he who comes to God must believe that he now notice that the there's a lot of uh uh italics here."],[1523.86,"Italics words are added"],[1526.86,"Uh for he who comes to God must believe that he is, and he is a rewarder of those who diligently seek him."],[1535.74,"Okay. So uh the way they'll want to cast this is again we have that believe there."],[1547.78,"Uh so they want to say, oh, you have believe that God exists."],[1551.94,"That's what it's talking about."],[1553.22,"Enoch was pleasing because he believed that God existed."],[1559.78,"Is there anyone else at that time who did so we're expected to believe that in the entire genealogy surrounding Enoch, this is Seth's genealogy."],[1569.18,"These are the faithful people."],[1571.34,"The other ones were atheists."],[1577.1,"The other ones didn't believe that God existed, and that's why they weren't taken away into heaven."],[1584.23,"So. So let me suggest that the word he is words he is in the context of the book of Genesis means something other than that that God as a person exists."],[1603.42,"What's God's name?"],[1606.42,"In Genesis? He's referred to as Yahweh, which comes from Ahiah, which is what he calls himself when he speaks to Moses."],[1610.8,"That means I am"],[1616.92,"Yahweh is basically he is."],[1620.68,"It's in a causative form, so it means he who causes to be."],[1624.36,"But breaking it down into Greek, it kind of means he is."],[1630.87,"So if we understand it that way."],[1632.97,"Without faithfulness, it's impossible to please God."],[1636.03,"What would that mean?"],[1637.55,"Well, it'd be like without faithfulness, it's impossible to please my wife."],[1642.43,"If I was unfaithful to my wife, it wouldn't matter if I brought her flowers when I got home from having an affair."],[1651.11,"It wouldn't matter if I gave her gifts, it wouldn't matter if I bought our car."],[1656.05,"If I'm not faithful to her, you didn't care about any of that."],[1663.71,"Do we have any examples of that?"],[1665.23,"Well, much of the old testament is about that."],[1668.05,"We just talked about how Abel pleased God with his sacrifice."],[1673.27,"And what do we find over and over again in the prophets in the Old Testament?"],[1677.19,"Israel is doing whatever they want, living wickedly, sinning, ignoring the commandments, but then going and offering the sacrifices."],[1685.91,"the And what does God send prophets to tell them?"],[1686.15,"sacrifices. I don't want your"],[1694.2,"Right? You honor me with your mouth, but your hearts are far away from me."],[1698.96,"So just stop it with the sacrifices."],[1701.78,"Until you mean it."],[1703.06,"That's what St."],[1703.94,"Paul is saying here."],[1706.5,"If you're not faithful to God, then it doesn't matter if you offer a sacrifice following the ritual that Abel followed."],[1715.61,"It's not just offering the right sacrifice."],[1718.81,"You have to be faithful."],[1720.17,"And then, when you're faithful, when you're a faithful disciple of God, then it becomes possible to please him."],[1732.19,"So Yahweh, the He is, is the covenant name of God."],[1736.84,"name. That's the God gives to his people"],[1738.24,"Meaning that's the name God gives to Israel."],[1745.28,"He doesn't broadcast that name to all the other countries."],[1750.7,"He reveals that to Moses."],[1755.47,"And that's not to say that no one before Moses had heard it, because it's used in the book of Genesis."],[1764.19,"But what we see over and over again is that, and this is also all through the Old Testament."],[1770.59,"God will use this phrase where he says, Then you will know that I am Yahweh."],[1777.73,"Then you will know that I am Yahweh."],[1779.33,"It's like, well, we we know your name."],[1782.63,"But whenever he says that, it's about something he's about to do."],[1787.59,"He's about to do something powerful."],[1791.79,"So, for example, he says it before the Exodus."],[1795.69,"Before like the plagues and the parting of the Red Sea."],[1798.73,"When you see this, you will know that I am Yahweh."],[1804.51,"At the other end of the Old Testament, he says in Ezekiel, the dry bones passage that we read on Pasca, he says to Israel, Then you will know that I am Yahweh when I have raised your dead out of the graves."],[1820.75,"So that's the idea here behind knowing that he is."],[1829.45,"This is the idea."],[1831.05,"So this is sort of the two pieces."],[1834.55,"He's breaking out faithfulness into two pieces."],[1838.84,"You have to know him."],[1841.69,"And he is, and he is a rewarder of those who diligently seek him."],[1851.69,"That's breaking faithlessness into two pieces."],[1855.69,"And when you do that, then you're able to please God."],[1863.61,"So, to put it another way, to use the metaphor again."],[1867.62,"I have to know my wife."],[1870.02,"I have to know who my wife is to be faithful to her, obviously."],[1875.8,"And then I also have to desire to get to know her better."],[1880.92,"That's the seek part here."],[1884.01,"That's the you will seek me and you will find me when you seek me with your whole heart."],[1892.73,"Those are the sort of the two pieces of faithfulness."],[1896.74,"It's not enough to just have one of those."],[1900.02,"It's not enough to just know God to have him reveal himself to you"],[1903.94,"That happened to everybody in Israel in the Old Testament."],[1906.5,"They all had the Torah"],[1906.86,"Had God revealed themselves to them, himself to them."],[1912.06,"They all had."],[1913.36,"That was available to all of them."],[1915.52,"They didn't all seek him."],[1918.8,"They didn't all follow him."],[1920.08,"They didn't all seek to draw close to him."],[1922.0,"And so that was the piece of faithfulness that they didn't have."],[1927.04,"It's not about believing that God exists."],[1928.8,"There were no atheists."],[1930.94,"At the time this was written, let alone in the ancient past."],[1938.46,"And there was nobody."],[1940.3,"The Israelites who are being condemned, the Israelites are out there worshiping Baal, didn't believe that Yahweh didn't exist."],[1947.18,"They were just worshiping other gods too."],[1949.26,"They were being unfaithful."],[1950.94,"They weren't atheists"],[1957.54,"Verse 7. By faith, Noah, being divinely warned of things not yet seen."],[1967.78,"We'll pause there."],[1972.02,"Notice was this a reference back to?"],[1975.54,"We just heard things not yet not seen."],[1979.54,"Back when we're talking about hope."],[1982.27,"Our hope, which has to do with the return of Christ, which has to do with judgment."],[1989.2,"Remember, that was brought up in chapter 10 in the context of not turning away from Christ."],[1994.48,"So St Not falling under judgment."],[1998.11,"Paul is kind of subtly here in the language making a comparison between us looking forward to Christ's return and the judgment that's going to happen there, and Noah being warned about the flood coming."],[2012.56,"Because we know Christ is returning, and Christ is returning to judge the living and the dead."],[2017.44,"We're able to prepare for that."],[2020.97,"We're able to prepare for that to happen in the same way that Noah was able to prepare for the flood."],[2026.97,"We don't need to build a big boat."],[2029.21,"Apologies to Ken Ham, who built a big boat."],[2032.01,"But we don't need to build a big boat."],[2033.77,"We have other work to do."],[2035.13,"Right. But um he built a big boat."],[2035.62,"But. And this, by the way this comparison is where you get all the comparisons of like the church to the ark."],[2047.6,"Noah built the ark, he built the boat."],[2050.26,"We build the church in preparation for Christ's return."],[2053.72,"This is sort of where that analogy flows from, and the rest of that symbolism comes in."],[2060.84,"Okay, so Noah's warned of things not yet seen, moved with godly fear."],[2067.09,"So he's not terrified and running around in a paddock."],[2069.97,"He's not holding up a sign, the end of the world is here to everybody."],[2074.45,"Um, but fear and reverence."],[2078.15,"The kind we should have when we think about Christ's return."],[2079.75,"This means I've got some work to do, to prepare"],[2085.75,"Prepared an ark for the saving of his household, by which he condemned the world and became heir of the righteousness which is according to faith."],[2098.63,"Again, what the righteousness which is according to faith."],[2103.19,"Righteousness comes from believing something is true."],[2106.2,"Um the righteousness, which is great due to faithfulness."],[2112.29,"But he condemned the world by what?"],[2116.93,"By turning away from it to build the ark."],[2122.39,"He that that meant that for the time he spent building the ark, he wasn't going out and engaging in commerce."],[2130.45,"He wasn't engaged in politics."],[2133.57,"He wasn't in all the things of the world."],[2135.49,"He had to leave those behind to prepare for the flood that was coming."],[2140.05,"That was coming to destroy that world."],[2142.77,"So the analogy here continues with Christ's return."],[2147.49,"That means we need to not be concerned about the things of the world."],[2150.53,"We need to be concerned about preparing ourselves, for Christ's return."],[2156.75,"That same parallel."],[2158.51,"Verse 8 I skipped over the Tower of Babel, because again, what?"],[2169.11,"That's not faithfulness."],[2171.81,"So there's nothing really there with faithfulness to talk about at the Tower of Babel."],[2175.49,"We go to Abraham."],[2177.81,"By faith Abraham obeyed when he was called to go out to the place which he would receive as an inheritance."],[2184.69,"And he went out not knowing where he was going."],[2188.37,"By faith he dwelt in the land of promise as in a foreign country, dwelling in tents with Isaac and Jacob, the heirs with him of the same promise."],[2197.09,"For he waited for the city which has foundations, whose builder and maker is God."],[2202.53,"Okay So by faith, Abraham obeyed."],[2211.29,"So this is saying, I guess, because he God comes and tells him, gives him a commitment, go leave everything and go to the other side of the country."],[2222.09,"And so I guess by faith would be he believed that that was actually God talking."],[2231.05,"So the other way to translate this by faith is just faithfully."],[2238.33,"Faithfully, Abraham obeyed when he was commanded to go out of the place."],[2244.38,"He was faithful in his obedience."],[2246.44,"That's what it's actually saying."],[2248.44,"That when God told him to do it, he did it."],[2251.45,"He didn't sit in Ur and just be like, Yes, I believe it is true that God is going to make a nation out of me and that he is going to give that land to my descendants."],[2260.85,"I believe it."],[2262.45,"Here in Ur."],[2264.66,"That's he is faithful by obeying the command."],[2269.46,"And he goes and he dwells in this land that's been promised to him, but he's dwelling in it as if it's a foreign country."],[2275.38,"Meaning he didn't just walk in and say, I own this place."],[2278.95,"But he that's where God told him to go."],[2281.35,"So he was faithful and he remained there."],[2284.96,"Even though he was living there as a foreigner."],[2287.78,"Meaning he didn't have power and authority and right over other people."],[2291.86,"He wasn't a citizen."],[2293.4,"None of that."],[2297.36,"He dwelt and dwelt in tents."],[2300.54,"Along with his sons, his son Isaac and his grandson Jacob."],[2305.32,"Who were the heirs with him of the same promise."],[2308.77,"All this was supposed to be theirs, but they're there living in tents."],[2312.47,"Not in the cities where the wealth is and everything."],[2315.27,"They left the biggest city in the world, the most powerful city in the world, or at the time."],[2320.23,"Because, why? Because St."],[2322.23,"Paul tells us, and you're not going to find this in the original, at least spelled out in Genesis, but St."],[2328.95,"Paul is telling us because he was waiting for the city which has foundations whose builder and maker is God."],[2337.83,"That city is not a city in the Old Testament."],[2339.51,"Because God didn't build that city"],[2341.39,"That's not talking about physical Jerusalem."],[2345.93,"That was a pagan city that David conquered."],[2351.53,"This is the city, the Jerusalem that St."],[2354.57,"John is going to talk about at the end of Revelation."],[2358.65,"That comes out of heaven, comes down out of heaven."],[2361.05,"That's how you know God is the one who laid its foundations."],[2365.59,"So that's the city that he's waiting for."],[2367.83,"That's why Abraham didn't dwell in cities of this world, because he was waiting for another city, that would that would come at the end."],[2378.69,"He was waiting for the promise"],[2381.46,"Notice that waiting."],[2384.19,"Remember that interplay of faithfulness and hope?"],[2388.03,"He has this hope that the promises are true that God has made to him."],[2393.55,"Promises that he's not going to see in this world."],[2396.43,"Abraham didn't see any of those promises fulfilled in this world."],[2401.79,"He had one son, but he did not see descendants as many as the sands of the seashore."],[2407.71,"He didn't see nations come from him."],[2410.19,"He didn't see any of his descendants become like the stars of heaven."],[2413.79,"He didn't see his descendants take possession of the land of Canaan."],[2418.64,"Okay. But he's waiting for that."],[2422.56,"Right after his death."],[2425.2,"He's waiting for it after his death."],[2426.32,"Paul sees this as implying resurrection"],[2427.48,"So St. But he's in the same position we are in terms of hope in the return of Christ."],[2435.88,"In fact, he's saying he has the same hope because we're hoping for that same city."],[2440.3,"We have the same hope."],[2441.34,"And while he's waiting, while he's hoping, what gives substance to that, what makes that real, is remaining faithful."],[2450.14,"When God gave him a command to leave and go to the other land, he went there."],[2454.62,"We told him to live there, he lived there."],[2457.2,"He was obedient, he was faithful."],[2459.69,"And then that gave substance to his hope."],[2467.53,"Verse 11. I'm just going to start translating by faith faithfully."],[2470.09,"Rather than because I'm belaboring it, I think"],[2475.49,"But so faithfully, Sarah herself also receives strength to conceive seed, and she bore a child when she was past the age, because she judged him faithful."],[2487.09,"Oh, look, there they translated it faithful."],[2490.93,"Who had promised."],[2493.97,"Okay. So when God's talking about it, right now, what if they translate it the other way?"],[2499.89,"She judged that God had faith."],[2502.13,"That would be."],[2505.05,"It's the same word."],[2508.39,"So, obviously that doesn't make sense, so we translate that as faithful."],[2512.89,"But what does this saying?"],[2515.03,"God made this promise that she was going to have a son, even though she was in her 90s."],[2521.83,"Okay. That doesn't happen."],[2523.43,"It didn't happen then normally."],[2525.59,"It doesn't happen now normally."],[2527.61,"So, but she judged that God would be faithful to that promise."],[2533.37,"And so she remained faithful to God."],[2539.54,"And because she remained faithful to God, that promise found its fulfillment."],[2546.9,"And she conceived and bore a son."],[2552.02,"Verse 12. Therefore, from one man, and him as good as dead, were born as the stars of the sky in multitude, innumerable as innumerable as the sand which is by the seashore."],[2569.58,"Okay, so not only is this translation bad and words are added, but it's punctuated poorly."],[2575.66,"Okay. So verse 12, therefore, from one man, Abraham is who we're talking about."],[2584.46,"Okay. From one man Abraham, and him as good as dead."],[2588.14,"What does it mean that he was as good as dead?"],[2589.82,"He was a hundred years old and had no children."],[2593.5,"Okay, that's a recipe for your family line dying out"],[2599.26,"The plan was, if you read in Genesis, before Sarah had Isaac, the plan was for him to designate one of his servants to inherit all his stuff."],[2612.9,"That was the plan."],[2614.82,"His most loyal, faithful servant was going to receive everything."],[2618.74,"So that's what it means by as good as dead."],[2621.06,"Family line done."],[2622.44,"Hundred years old, no deal."],[2624.04,"No children. But so from him were born, drop out the italicized word."],[2630.92,"We're born as the stars of the sky, comma"],[2638.28,"In number or in multitude, innumerable as the sand which is by the seashore."],[2644.76,"It's actually written"],[2645.41,"So this is the way in Genesis 15."],[2651.39,"That as many as is getting added here."],[2654.45,"So the promise was twofold."],[2656.61,"Number one, there'll be as many, what quantity."],[2660.77,"And then number two, as the stars of the heavens."],[2663.97,"That's not quantity, that's quality."],[2666.93,"That's what your descendants are going to become like."],[2670.05,"And the stars of the heavens in the ancient world, in the Ur III period, when Abraham lived, these are gods for the pagans."],[2680.05,"These are angels and angelic beings for the Torah."],[2686.47,"Like the stars of heavens."],[2688.04,"So your children are going to become, like vast in number."],[2695.31,"But also they're going to become like the angels of heaven."],[2699.79,"This is what we call theosis."],[2701.47,"This is becoming a saint."],[2703.63,"Salvation. Salvation. Having a lot of kids who all die and go to hell is not much of a promise."],[2713.03,"The salvation element is an important part of the promises to Abraham."],[2716.71,"I don't think that's going out on a limb."],[2718.57,"Salvation is an important part of that."],[2720.09,"Not just you'll have lots of kids."],[2722.01,"They'll all go awry."],[2723.79,"Like that's um."],[2727.61,"And so both of those are there."],[2728.97,"Because what St."],[2730.05,"Paul is keeping, we've seen all the way through here, he's making this connection."],[2734.77,"He set out what he's doing at the beginning."],[2736.93,"Talking about faithfulness and this hope."],[2739.81,"This hope of salvation at Christ's return."],[2742.69,"So we had the city from heaven and the last rose."],[2744.93,"Here it's both of these parts."],[2748.1,"But there's faithfulness."],[2749.46,"Sarah is faithful because she knows God will be faithful."],[2753.62,"The result is this larger hope, of salvation."],[2759.45,"Salvation. Abraham is the father of all who are faithful."],[2764.67,"Everyone who is faithful has Abraham for a father."],[2767.97,"That's St. Paul in Romans and Galatians."],[2771.41,"Verse 13 These all died, these all died faithfully, in faithfulness, not having received the promises, but having seen them afar off, were assured of them, embraced them, and confessed that they were strangers and pilgrims on the earth."],[2798.89,"So again, these all died believing it was true."],[2806.43,"Not having received them."],[2808.19,"That sounds like a tragic story."],[2811.88,"That almost makes it sound like it's saying that they were wrong."],[2815.48,"They all believed it was true, but they died without receiving any of it"],[2822.49,"That's not right."],[2824.65,"Is that what it's saying?"],[2825.93,"Saying, even though, as we just mentioned, Abraham didn't in this world, receive any of those things or see any of those things, he remained faithful till the day he died."],[2838.85,"Because remember, chapter 10, that's what St."],[2842.37,"Paul was very obviously talking about."],[2844.29,"Telling the people they needed to remain faithful until the end, not apostasize, not fall away, not turn away from Christ."],[2853.43,"And so this is what Abraham and Isaac and Jacob and Sarah are examples of here."],[2858.07,"They're people who lived faithfully their whole lives"],[2864.75,"Just like all the people to whom St."],[2866.91,"Paul was talking."],[2870.8,"Did not see the return of Christ during their earthly life."],[2877.12,"They lived and died without seeing it in this world."],[2882.21,"But hopefully, St."],[2883.57,"Paul, what St."],[2884.69,"Paul was urging them on towards was remaining faithful until the end, despite that."],[2889.49,"The fact that they hadn't seen it, didn't mean it wasn't true."],[2893.11,"Doesn't mean God wasn't going to be faithful to do it."],[2896.63,"We talked about that at the end of chapter 10, about God delaying that, but not forever."],[2908.4,"And so they embraced and confessed that they were."],[2913.44,"Again, that them is added."],[2915.04,"They embraced and confessed that they were strangers and pilgrims on the earth."],[2922.72,"Meaning they weren't looking for anything here."],[2926.64,"They were looking for something beyond here."],[2930.4,"Even up until the day they died, even though they didn't receive that thing yet."],[2940.04,"Verse 14, for those who say such things declare plainly that they seek a homeland."],[2947.24,"And truly, if they had called to mind that from that country, that from which they had come out, they would have had opportunity to return."],[2957.72,"But now they desired a better, that is a heavenly country."],[2961.56,"Therefore, God is not ashamed to be called their God, for he has prepared a city for them."],[2969.24,"So those who say such things."],[2975.69,"Um the idea is they're they're seeking a homeland, a city."],[2981.14,"A place where they belong."],[2982.82,"Because if you declare here where I am right now, I am a stranger, I'm a wanderer, I'm a nomad."],[2989.2,"You're saying this isn't my home."],[2992.74,"That my home is somewhere else."],[2994.82,"That's sort of de facto."],[2996.44,"What that means."],[2998.09,"And if they had wanted, you're saying Abraham could have gone back to Ur."],[3004.77,"Which was his homeland, it's where he was born."],[3007.95,"So could Isaac."],[3009.47,"They went back to Mesopotamia to get a wife for Isaac, they could have gone back and lived there."],[3014.19,"Jacob could have Jacob went back there to work for his uncle for a while."],[3018.11,"He could have stayed there."],[3019.61,"So if they were just looking for an earthly homeland, they could have gone back to it."],[3023.71,"They could have had it."],[3024.85,"So that means if they're a nomad living as a nomad and a stranger, so this is not their home."],[3033.13,"And the earthly place that they could have called their home was not their home."],[3037.37,"That means they're looking for some homeland beyond this world."],[3042.02,"Beyond the material, physical world in which we currently live, beyond this age."],[3047.22,"That's what they're looking for."],[3049.16,"So St. Paul is showing how that's in the text in Genesis."],[3054.06,"This isn't some allegory."],[3056.22,"This is something weird where we're analogizing."],[3059.52,"Where really what the Old Testament is talking about is Palestine."],[3064.16,"It's the land of Canaan."],[3065.52,"And we're now making an analogy to heaven."],[3068.53,"Though St. Paul's showing how no, this is built into the text."],[3072.65,"This is built into what it says in Genesis is that Abraham is looking for something beyond this age in this world."],[3080.18,"Not just for his biological descendants to claim a piece of physical territory in this world, in this age."],[3087.78,"That's not what he was looking for."],[3092.82,"And so it is because it is because that's what they're seeking."],[3100.58,"Because they're seeking God Himself."],[3102.76,"As their inheritance."],[3103.96,"They're not seeking something on this earth."],[3105.88,"That is why God is not ashamed to be called their God."],[3110.36,"For he has prepared a city for them."],[3111.88,"He has prepared that thing they're hoping for for them."],[3116.8,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[3124.16,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3129.52,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.56,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: Hebrews as sermon, apostasy warnings","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up <b>Hebrews 11</b>; left off end of <b>ch. 10</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrews = not really an epistle - a <b>homily/sermon</b> sent somewhere</li><li class=\"d1\">no letter-opening in ch. 1; letter-type closing stuff comes in <b>ch. 13</b></li><li class=\"d1\">more evidence tonight of <b>oral delivery</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Recurring theme: <b>warnings against apostasy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">turning from Christ under <b>persecution</b> or for <b>things of the world</b></li><li class=\"d0\">English (NKJV) says <b>faith</b> - vague; Protestants read theology in</li><li class=\"d1\">better: <b>faithfulness</b> - contrast is faithfulness vs <b>betrayal</b>, natural opposites</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"believing something true\" vs sin - intellectual vs holistic doesn't match</li><li class=\"d0\">Ch. 10 ended: remain faithful <b>until the end</b> - endurance, patience in suffering</li></ul>"},{"t":220.26,"lv":2,"h":"Why chapter 11: what faithfulness looks like","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ch. 11 = <b>expounds what faithfulness is</b> - looks like concretely</li><li class=\"d0\">OT frames God-Israel relation as <b>marriage</b> - faithful/unfaithful</li><li class=\"d1\">still abstract: what does loyalty mean \"driving my car… <b>watching Netflix</b>\"?</li><li class=\"d0\">Method: <b>examples from the OT</b></li><li class=\"d1\">mostly <b>Genesis</b>, then Exodus, <b>Joshua</b>, brushes Judges - no further</li><li class=\"d1\">figures who showed faithfulness in some circumstance or whole life - <b>example to us</b></li></ul>"},{"t":353.23,"lv":3,"h":"Saints: why not just Christ?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Where Orthodox rub against <b>Protestant friends</b>: <b>venerating saints</b>, icons, hymns</li><li class=\"d0\">Objection: \"why not <b>pray directly to Christ</b>?\" Why the <b>Theotokos</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">inconsistent - they ask <b>pastor and friends to pray</b> for them</li><li class=\"d0\">Not the scriptural pattern anyway</li><li class=\"d1\">OT is about Christ but he is <b>not the main character</b> - Genesis follows Adam, Cain, Abel</li><li class=\"d1\">\"why not just the Gospels?\" - why <b>Acts</b>, Peter, Paul, Philip?</li><li class=\"d1\">why the \"crazy, violent stuff\" of OT figures?</li><li class=\"d0\">Whole ch. 11 points to <b>OT saints as models</b> for Christian life - verse at end of chapter always comes up</li></ul>"},{"t":546.37,"lv":3,"h":"Church does what Paul does","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul does with OT saints exactly what the Church does with <b>lives of the saints</b></li><li class=\"d1\">read them, see how they lived Christian life, <b>example and encouragement</b></li><li class=\"d1\">commemorating on days, hearing what they did - <b>followed since the beginning</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not putting saint <b>alongside Christ</b>; not \"this person will save you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">their life helps us <b>know Christ more deeply</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Even earlier: every preacher preaching <b>Peter or Paul from Acts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">preceding saints \"not apart from Christ\" but leading us to him, as when alive</li></ul>"},{"t":677.82,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 11:1-2 - Faith as hypostasis","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>11:1</b> \"faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen\" - translation \"made it make no sense\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><i>hypostasis</i> = <b>person</b> in Trinity doctrine - one essence, three <i>hypostases</i></li><li class=\"d1\">means a <b>particular being</b>, an entity - localized thing</li><li class=\"d0\">Hope in Hebrews = <b>Christ's return, glorification, eternal life</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Faithfulness = <b>substance, reality</b> of that hope</li><li class=\"d1\">\"believing it's true\" is just what hope <b>is</b> - adds nothing</li><li class=\"d1\">actually <b>living faithfully makes hope real</b></li></ul>"},{"t":825.63,"lv":3,"h":"Backwards modern approach","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Moderns: <b>\"prove it\"</b> - eternal life, Jesus is God - then believe, then maybe live faithfully</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: <b>exact opposite</b></li><li class=\"d1\">certainty comes by <b>living faithfully</b> - how we know and see Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">life of <b>repentance</b>, doing good, loving one another</li><li class=\"d1\">we see <b>ourselves and others transformed</b> → gives hope substance</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Evidence of things not seen\" = same thing</li><li class=\"d0\">Like <b>John the Forerunner</b> in prison: \"are you the one?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ: lame walk, blind see, <b>sins forgiven</b> - look at what's happening</li><li class=\"d0\">Sign-seekers Christ condemns: \"do a magic trick… <b>what have you done for me lately</b>\" - exactly in reverse</li></ul>"},{"t":1041.86,"lv":3,"h":"11:2 - good testimony","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>11:2</b> elders obtained a <b>good testimony</b></li><li class=\"d1\">image used before: dying person enters God's presence, <b>introduced by Christ</b> with angels and saints</li><li class=\"d1\">testimony = <b>who you've become</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Achieved by faithfulness - the saints had our hope \"in our place\"</li><li class=\"d1\">looking at them = <b>model for achieving</b> that hope</li></ul>"},{"t":1119.06,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 11:3-4 - creation, Abel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>11:3</b> worlds framed by the Word of God - \"by faith we understand\"</li><li class=\"d1\">believing true doesn't bring understanding; <b>faithfulness</b> brings understanding that God is creator</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Adam and Eve skipped</b> - not good examples of faithfulness</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>11:4</b> <b>Abel</b> - more excellent sacrifice than Cain</li><li class=\"d1\">first human <b>saint</b>, first human to <b>die</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>God himself</b> testified of his gifts - Genesis: sacrifice acceptable</li><li class=\"d0\">Protestant line: Abel righteous because he <b>had faith</b> - but text says God testified of his <b>gift</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he <b>did something</b> - offered worthy sacrifice, <b>faithfully</b></li><li class=\"d1\">unclear what Abel would have \"believed\" - can't separate faith from the work</li></ul>"},{"t":1359.25,"lv":3,"h":"Pistis translation; Abel still speaks","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><i>pistis</i> and forms → faith, faithful, faithfulness, <b>belief, believe</b> in English</li><li class=\"d1\">some places can't avoid faithfulness (last time); elsewhere <b>theological reasons</b> for faith/belief</li><li class=\"d1\">faith can carry \"<b>kept faith</b> with his wife\"; <b>belief</b> can't - not even related in modern English</li><li class=\"d0\">\"He being dead <b>still speaks</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">for friends who say saints can't pray because dead - \"<b>here's the first one</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1457.66,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 11:5-6 - Enoch, and \"he is\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>11:5</b> <b>Enoch</b> taken so he did not see death - testimony he <b>pleased God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">faithful, lived pleasing life, God took him without dying</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>11:6</b> without faith impossible to please him; must believe \"that he is\" - italics = <b>added words</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Protestant read: <b>believe God exists</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then everyone else in <b>Seth's genealogy</b> was an atheist?</li><li class=\"d0\">\"He is\" = <b>Yahweh</b> from <i>Ehyeh</i> = I am</li><li class=\"d1\">causative: \"he who causes to be\"; in Greek \"he is\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Analogy: without faithfulness impossible to please <b>my wife</b></li><li class=\"d1\">flowers after an affair mean nothing</li></ul>"},{"t":1663.71,"lv":3,"h":"Prophets: sacrifices without faithfulness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">OT proof: Israel lives wickedly but <b>offers sacrifices</b></li><li class=\"d1\">prophets: \"<b>I don't want your sacrifices</b>\" - honor me with mouths, hearts far</li><li class=\"d0\">Not just right ritual (Abel's) - <b>must be faithful</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then faithful disciple can <b>please him</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1732.19,"lv":3,"h":"Yahweh - knowing that he is","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Yahweh = <b>covenant name</b> given to Israel, revealed to Moses - not broadcast to other nations</li><li class=\"d0\">Phrase: \"then you will know that <b>I am Yahweh</b>\" - always before something powerful</li><li class=\"d1\">before <b>Exodus</b>, plagues, Red Sea</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Ezekiel</b> dry bones (Pascha): \"when I have <b>raised your dead</b> out of the graves\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Faithfulness in <b>two pieces</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>know him</b> - he is</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>diligently seek him</b> - like knowing my wife and wanting to know her better</li><li class=\"d1\">\"seek me with your whole heart\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not enough to have one: all Israel had the <b>Torah</b>, not all <b>sought</b> him</li><li class=\"d0\">No <b>atheists</b> then - Baal worshipers believed Yahweh existed, just worshiped others too → <b>unfaithful</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1957.54,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 11:7 - Noah and the ark","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>11:7</b> Noah \"warned of things <b>not yet seen</b>\" - echoes hope, Christ's return, <b>judgment</b> (ch. 10)</li><li class=\"d1\">we know Christ returns to judge → <b>prepare</b> as Noah did</li><li class=\"d1\">no big boat needed - \"apologies to <b>Ken Ham</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Source of <b>church = ark</b> analogy - we build the church for Christ's return</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Godly fear</b>\" - not panic with a sign, but reverence; \"I've got work to do\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Condemned the world\" by <b>turning away</b> from it to build</li><li class=\"d1\">no commerce, politics, things of the world</li><li class=\"d1\">so for us: <b>not concerned with the world</b>, preparing for return</li><li class=\"d0\">Righteousness \"according to faith\" = due to <b>faithfulness</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2158.51,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 11:8-10 - Abraham, the city","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Tower of Babel</b> skipped - no faithfulness there</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>11:8</b> Abraham obeyed, went out not knowing where</li><li class=\"d1\">better: <b>faithfully obeyed</b> - didn't sit in <b>Ur</b> saying \"I believe\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Dwelt in promised land <b>as foreign country</b>, in <b>tents</b> with Isaac, Jacob - heirs of same promise</li><li class=\"d1\">no power, no citizenship; left the <b>biggest, most powerful city</b> of the time</li><li class=\"d0\">Waiting for city \"<b>whose builder and maker is God</b>\" - not in Genesis spelled out</li><li class=\"d1\">not physical Jerusalem - <b>pagan city David conquered</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>Jerusalem of Revelation</b>, coming down out of heaven</li></ul>"},{"t":2378.69,"lv":3,"h":"Abraham's hope and resurrection","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Abraham saw <b>none of the promises</b> fulfilled in this world</li><li class=\"d1\">one son; no sands-of-seashore descendants, no nations, no stars, no <b>land of Canaan</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Waiting <b>after death</b> → Paul sees <b>resurrection</b> implied</li><li class=\"d0\">Same position as us: <b>same hope</b>, same city</li><li class=\"d0\">What gives substance to hope = <b>faithful obedience</b></li><li class=\"d1\">told to leave → left; told to live there → lived</li></ul>"},{"t":2467.53,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 11:11-12 - Sarah, stars and sand","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>11:11</b> Sarah - \"she judged him <b>faithful</b>\" - here translators use faithful, same word</li><li class=\"d1\">in her 90s, doesn't happen then or now; she judged God would be faithful → <b>remained faithful</b> → fulfilled</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>11:12</b> \"from one man, as good as dead\" - translation and punctuation poor</li><li class=\"d1\">Abraham 100, no children; plan was to leave all to <b>chief servant</b> - \"family line done\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Drop italic word: born <b>as the stars of the sky, comma</b>, in multitude, innumerable as sand</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Genesis 15</b>: promise twofold - quantity (<b>sand</b>) and quality (<b>stars</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">stars: <b>gods</b> for pagans (Ur III), <b>angels</b> in Torah → children become like angels = <b>theosis</b>, salvation</li><li class=\"d1\">\"lots of kids who all go to hell is not much of a promise\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Abraham <b>father of all who are faithful</b> - Romans, Galatians</li></ul>"},{"t":2771.41,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 11:13 - died faithfully","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>11:13</b> \"all died in faith, not having received the promises\" - sounds tragic, as if they were wrong</li><li class=\"d1\">not so: they remained <b>faithful until the day they died</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ties to <b>ch. 10</b>: remain faithful to the end, no apostasy</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's audience also hadn't seen <b>Christ's return</b> in their lives</li><li class=\"d1\">not seeing it doesn't mean it isn't true - <b>God delaying, not forever</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Them\" added: <b>embraced and confessed</b> strangers and pilgrims on earth</li><li class=\"d1\">looking for something <b>beyond here</b>, even without receiving it</li></ul>"},{"t":2940.04,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 11:14-16 - seeking a homeland","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>11:14-16</b> they seek a <b>homeland</b>; could have returned; desired a <b>heavenly country</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Declaring \"I'm a stranger, a nomad\" = <b>this isn't my home</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Abraham could have gone back to <b>Ur</b>; Isaac's wife from Mesopotamia; <b>Jacob</b> worked there for uncle</li><li class=\"d1\">earthly home was available → so they sought <b>beyond this age</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not allegory - Paul shows it's <b>built into Genesis</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just \"Palestine\" then analogy to heaven</li><li class=\"d1\">not just descendants claiming a piece of <b>territory in this age</b></li><li class=\"d0\">They seek <b>God himself</b> as inheritance</li><li class=\"d1\">so God is <b>not ashamed to be called their God</b> - has <b>prepared a city</b> for them</li></ul>"}]});
